Let me look at the `_gnutls_x509_decode_string` function again. I think the bug is there:

```c
ret = make_printable_string(etype, &td, output);
if (ret == GNUTLS_E_INVALID_REQUEST) {  /* unsupported etype */
    output->data = td.data;
    output->size = td.size;
    ret = 0;
} else if (ret <= 0) {
    _gnutls_free_datum(&td);
}
```

When `make_printable_string` returns a negative error (like `GNUTLS_E_MEMORY_ERROR`), `ret <= 0` is true, so `td` is freed. But `output` is left uninitialized. Then:

```c
if (etype != ASN1_ETYPE_OCTET_STRING) {
    if (output->data)  // UNINITIALIZED READ!
```

This is a classic MSAN issue! But the crash state says `decode_complex_string`, not `_gnutls_x509_decode_string`.

Wait, let me re-read the crash state: "decode_complex_string _gnutls_x509_dn_to_string append_elements"

This means the call stack is:
1. `append_elements` calls `_gnutls_x509_dn_to_string`
2. `_gnutls_x509_dn_to_string` calls `decode_complex_string`
3. The crash is in `decode_complex_string`

So the uninitialized value is used in `decode_complex_string` itself. Let me look at it again:

```c
if (etype != ASN1_ETYPE_INVALID) {
    result = make_printable_string(etype, &td, out);
    _gnutls_free_datum(&td);
    if (result < 0)
        return gnutls_assert_val(result);
} else {
    out->data = td.data;
    out->size = td.size;
}

assert(out->data != NULL);

/* Refuse to deal with strings containing NULs. */
if (strlen((void *) out->data) != (size_t) out->size) {
    _gnutls_free_datum(out);
    return gnutls_assert_val(GNUTLS_E_ASN1_EMBEDDED_NULL_IN_STRING);
}
```

When `make_printable_string` returns 0, `out` is properly set. When it returns < 0, we return early. When `etype == ASN1_ETYPE_INVALID`, `out` is set from `td`.

But wait - what if `make_printable_string` returns 0 but `out->data` is NULL? Let me check the `ASN1_ETYPE_BMP_STRING` case:

```c
if (etype == ASN1_ETYPE_BMP_STRING) {
    ret = _gnutls_ucs2_to_utf8(input->data, input->size, out, 1);
    if (ret < 0) {
        printable = 0;
    } else
        printable = 1;
}
```

If `_gnutls_ucs2_to_utf8` returns 0 (success), `printable = 1`, and we skip `data2hex`. `out` is properly set by `_gnutls_ucs2_to_utf8`.

If `_gnutls_ucs2_to_utf8` returns < 0 (failure), `printable = 0`, and `data2hex` is called. `out` is properly set by `data2hex`.

So `out` should always be set when `make_printable_string` returns 0.

Hmm, let me look at the `_gnutls_ucs2_to_utf8` function one more time:

```c
int _gnutls_ucs2_to_utf8(const void *data, size_t size,
                         gnutls_datum_t * output, unsigned be)
{
    int ret;
    size_t dstlen;
    void *src;
    uint8_t *tmp_dst = NULL;
    uint8_t *dst = NULL;

    if (size > 2 && ((uint8_t *) data)[size-1] == 0 && ((uint8_t *) data)[size-2] == 0) {
        size -= 2;
    }

    if (size == 0)
        return gnutls_assert_val(GNUTLS_E_INVALID_REQUEST);

    src = gnutls_malloc(size+2);
    if (src == NULL)
        return gnutls_assert_val(GNUTLS_E_MEMORY_ERROR);

    change_u16_endianness(src, data, size, be);

    dstlen = 0;
    tmp_dst = u16_to_u8((uint16_t*)src, size/2, NULL, &dstlen);
    if (tmp_dst == NULL) {
        ret = gnutls_assert_val(GNUTLS_E_MEMORY_ERROR);
        goto fail;
    }

    dst = gnutls_malloc(dstlen+1);
    if (dst == NULL) {
        gnutls_assert();
        ret = GNUTLS_E_MEMORY_ERROR;
        goto fail;
    }

    memcpy(dst, tmp_dst, dstlen);
    dst[dstlen] = 0;

    output->data = (void *) dst;
    output->size = dstlen;

    ret = 0;
    goto cleanup;

 fail:
    gnutls_free(dst);

 cleanup:
    gnutls_free(src);
    free(tmp_dst);

    return ret;
}
```

When `u16_to_u8` fails (returns NULL), `tmp_dst` is NULL, and we go to `fail`. `dst` is NULL, `gnutls_free(NULL)` is a no-op. Then in `cleanup`, `free(NULL)` is a no-op. `output` is NOT modified. So `out` remains uninitialized.

Then in `make_printable_string`, `printable` stays 0, and `data2hex` is called. `data2hex` allocates `out->data` and sets `out->size`. So `out` is properly set.

